data.content:
The lightest approach for .NET 8+ is the built-in **`TimeProvider`** class — no DI container or third-party packages needed. You just accept it as a constructor parameter with a default:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
        => _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In production you change nothing (the default gives real time). In tests you pass `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider` to freeze or advance time deterministically.

**Why this is the lightest option:**
- `TimeProvider` ships in the BCL (no extra NuGet for the abstraction)
- No interface to define yourself, no DI container to wire up
- The optional parameter means existing callers don't break

